<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hedeby</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Haithabu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hedeby was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> trading town at the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schlei">Schlei</a> inlet in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a>, now in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. From the eighth to the eleventh century it was one of the largest towns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, linking Baltic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> trade and protected by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danevirke">Danevirke</a>. It was sacked around 1050 and again in 1066, after which the town of Schleswig replaced it. Excavations have made it a major archaeological site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hedeby.jpg" alt="Hedeby" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kai-Erik Ballak · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wikingerhaeuser_Haithabu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
